[ChapterTitle "The ...c5 and ...Qb6 lines"]
[DefaultTags "vs-c5"]

{# The ...c5 and ...Qb6 lines

This is the chapter to know cold. Everything else in the London can be played on general ideas, but here Black has a real try and White needs an answer ready.

## What Black is doing

Two moves work together. ...c5 attacks d4, the base of White's triangle. ...Qb6 attacks b2, which lost its defender the moment the bishop went to f4. If White answers both moves passively, Black gets a comfortable game for free.

## The rule to remember

There is only one rule, and it depends on whether the c-pawn has moved yet.

If ...Qb6 arrives before White has played c3, answer Nc3. White is offering the b2 pawn on purpose. If Black takes, the knight jumps to b5, threatens Nc7 forking king and rook, and Black's queen sits on b2 doing nothing while White develops with threats. Most club opponents take the pawn and regret it.

If ...Qb6 arrives after White has played c3, answer Qb3. The c2 square is empty, so the diagonal is open, and offering the trade kills Black's most active piece. After ...Qxb3 recapture with axb3 and enjoy the open a-file.

## Be honest about the pawn

The Nc3 line is a real pawn offer, not a trap with a forced win at the end. What White gets is development, threats and open lines against a queen that has wandered. Play it for the initiative and keep making threats. If Black gives the pawn back to finish developing, take it and be happy.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Panicking and playing b3, which weakens the queenside for the rest of the game. Playing dxc5 to dodge the pressure, which hands Black the centre. Playing c3 or e3 while b2 is hanging and simply losing the pawn for nothing. Chasing the queen with Rb1 before the knight has jumped, which lets it escape to a2.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 c5
{Black hits the base of the triangle before White has built it. This is the most testing practical try at club level. [%weight 7]}
( 2... c6
{A quieter move that also frees the queen's path to b6. The ...Qb6 that follows needs a different answer. [%weight 4]}
3. e3
{Build the triangle as usual. Black has not threatened anything yet. [%tags development]}
3... Qb6 {The same hit on b2, but with the pawn on c6 rather than c5. [%weight 5]} 4. Qc1
{Here the knight jump does not work: with a pawn on c6, Nc3 and Nb5 is met by ...cxb5 and White is simply a piece down. Defend b2 with the queen instead. The Qb3 trade offer is not available yet, because the pawn on c2 blocks the diagonal. [%tags vs-qb6] [%cal Rc6b5]}
( 4. Nc3?
{The idea from the ...c5 lines fails here. After ...Qxb2 the knight cannot go to b5, because the pawn on c6 takes it.}
) ( 4. b3?!
{It holds b2 but leaves the queenside dark squares weak, and the bishop on f4 loses its retreat.}
) 4... Nf6
{With b2 defended, the queen sortie is over for now, so Black develops normally. [%weight 7]}
5. Nf3
{Develop normally. Black's queen on b6 is hitting nothing and will have to move again before the rooks connect. [%tags development] [%plan Play c3, Bd3 and castle. Once the pawn reaches c3 the Qb3 trade offer is available whenever you want it, and Black's queen has to keep watching for it.] [%line ...c6 and ...Qb6]}
( 5. c4?!
{It opens the position while White's queen sits awkwardly on c1 and the king is still in the centre.}
) ) ( 2... Nf6
{The knight first, and ...c5 comes next: the Slav course's order into the same lines. [%weight 5]}
3. e3 {Build the triangle as always. [%tags development]} 3... c5
{Hitting d4, and it is chapter 2's subject by another order. [%weight 7]} 4. Nf3
{Develop, and it is the position after 2...c5 3.e3 Nf6 4.Nf3: the same position, with ...e6 or ...Nc6 to come. [%tags development] [%plan The ...c5 lines from here: c3 against ...Nc6, and the ...e6 setup as in the ...Nf6 first line.] [%line ...Nf6 and then ...c5]}
( 4. dxc5?! {It gives up the centre for a pawn Black takes back with 4...e6 or 4...Qa5+.}
) ( 4. Nc3?! {It blocks the c-pawn that holds d4.} ) ) 3. e3
{Hold the centre and finish the setup. The d4 pawn cannot be won by force, so there is nothing to panic about. [%tags vs-c5 development]}
( 3. dxc5?!
{It gives up the centre. Black recaptures with the bishop or the e-pawn and has an easy game.}
) ( 3. c3!?
{Sound, and it is where the pawn is going anyway. Playing e3 first keeps White's options open for one more move.}
) ( 3. Nf3!? {Also fine. Either move order works as long as the triangle gets built.} ) (
3. e4?!
{Opening the centre with the king at home and Black already hitting d4. Black comes out slightly on top, though it is not a disaster.}
) 3... Qb6
{The critical move of the whole repertoire. The queen hits b2, which the bishop left undefended when it went to f4. [%weight 5]}
( 3... Nc6
{The most common development. Black adds pressure to d4 before deciding on a setup. [%weight 7]}
4. Nf3
{Develop the kingside knight and leave the other one at home. Keeping b1 free means ...Qb6 can still be met by Nc3. [%tags development]}
( 4. c3!?
{Perfectly sound, and it is the move White wants eventually. Playing the knight first keeps the Nc3 answer to ...Qb6 available for one more move.}
) ( 4. Nc3?!
{Here the knight takes the c-pawn's square with no ...Qb6 on the board to justify it.} ) (
4. Bb5?!
{The pin does nothing. Black plays ...Qa5+ or ...a6 and White has moved the bishop to a square it did not want.}
) ( 4. dxc5?! {It hands Black the centre and a free tempo with ...e6 and ...Bxc5.} )
4... Nf6
{Both sides just develop. Black keeps every option open for one more move. [%weight 7]} (
4... cxd4
{Releasing the tension. It helps White, who gets a free open file and a strong centre. [%weight 5]}
5. exd4
{Take towards the centre. The pawn on d4 is now backed by the c-pawn and the e-file is open for a rook. [%tags development]}
( 5. Nxd4?!
{It puts the knight where Black wants to hit it with ...e5, and gives up the strong pawn centre for nothing.}
) 5... Nf6
{Black develops against the new pawn centre. The open e-file belongs to White, so Black has to catch up quickly. [%weight 7]}
6. c3
{Support the d4 pawn and get on with the usual setup. [%tags development] [%plan Play Bd3, Nbd2 and castle, then consider Ne5 or the e4 break. The open e-file suits White, whose pieces get there first.] [%line ...Nc6 and an early ...cxd4]}
) 5. c3
{Now complete the triangle. With the pawn on c3 an eventual ...Qb6 can be answered by Qb3, offering the trade. [%tags development] [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3]}
( 5. Nc3?!
{The knight is on the pawn's square again, and d4 ends up with one defender too few.} ) (
5. dxc5?!
{There is no reason to release the tension while Black still has pieces at home.} )
5... Qb6 {The queen sortie one move too late. [%weight 4]} ( 5... e6
{Back to a classical setup, with the c-pawn already committed. [%weight 7]} 6. Nbd2
{The knight takes its usual square now that the c-pawn has gone to c3. [%tags development]}
( 6. Ne5?!
{Too early. Black answers ...Nxe5 and White has to recapture before the support is in place.}
) ( 6. Bd3!?
{Fine, but the knight first avoids ...Nh5 or ...cxd4 tricks while the bishop is loose.} )
6... Bd6 {The trade offer, exactly as in chapter 1. [%weight 6]} 7. Bg3
{Keep the bishop, as always. If Black takes, recapture with the h-pawn and take the open file. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 7. Bxd6?! {It gives Black a free developing move and gets rid of White's best piece.} )
7... O-O
{Black castles, and the position looks like chapter 1 with the c-pawn already committed. [%weight 7]}
8. Bd3
{The bishop takes aim at h7 now that Black has committed the king. [%tags bishop-placement kingside-attack] [%cal Bd3h7]}
( 8. Be2?! {There is no pawn on g6, so the bishop should be on the diagonal that matters.}
) 8... b6
{The light-squared bishop heads for b7, the standard way out from behind the e6 wall. [%weight 5]}
9. O-O {King safety before anything else. The attack keeps. [%tags king-safety]} (
9. Ne5!?
{Not wrong, but castling first means an eventual ...cxd4 never comes with a check or a pin.}
) ( 9. h4?
{The king is still in the centre and the h-pawn is not what attacks. Black opens the centre with ...cxd4 and White is in trouble.}
) 9... Bb7
{The bishop lands on the long diagonal. Black's pieces point at the queenside and centre, White's at the kingside. [%weight 7]}
10. Ne5
{The outpost, fully supported by c3, d4 and the knight on d2. This is the position the whole chapter was aiming at. [%tags kingside-attack] [%plan Play f4, then lift the rook with Rf3 and Rh3 and bring the queen across. Watch for ...cxd4 opening the c-file towards your own king, and recapture with the c-pawn when it helps.] [%line ...Nc6 first, and the full setup] [%csl Ge5] [%cal Rf2f4]}
( 10. e4?!
{It opens the centre exactly when Black's pieces are ready for it. Keep the centre closed and play on the kingside.}
) ) 6. Qb3
{With the pawn on c3 the diagonal is open, so offer the trade. Black's most active piece disappears and White gets the a-file. [%tags vs-qb6] [%cal Gd1b3]}
( 6. Nbd2?
{b2 is hanging. Black takes it, and proving compensation without the Nc3 and Nb5 idea is much harder.}
) ( 6. b3?!
{It holds the pawn but leaves the dark squares around the queenside weak for the rest of the game.}
) ( 6. Qc1!? {Safe but passive, and it undoes the point of having played c3.} ) 6... Qxb3
{Black takes, which is the consistent choice: the queen came out to be active, and retreating now would hand back the time it cost. [%weight 6]}
( 6... c4
{The pawn hits the queen instead of trading, which is what the Slav course plays here: it takes b3 from the queen and fixes the queenside, and the trade that follows is on Black's terms, with the a-file opened. [%weight 5]}
7. Qxb6
{Take, because the queen has nowhere better: 7.Qc2 Bf5 hits it again, and 7.Qd1 Qxb2 loses the pawn. After 7...axb6 the queenside is a little loose for Black and the game is level, with the pawn on c4 a target for b3 later. [%tags development] [%plan 7...axb6 8.Nbd2, then Be2, O-O and b3 to hit the pawn on c4. Level; the pawn on c4 is the target and the doubled b-pawns are Black's price for the a-file.] [%line ...Qb6 and ...c4, the queens off]}
( 7. Qd1? {7...Qxb2 and the pawn has gone with the knight on b1 pinned to the rook.} ) (
7. Bxc4?
{7...dxc4 and the bishop has gone for a pawn: the queen on b3 does not take back on c4 with the queen on b6 hitting it.}
) ( 7. Qc2!?
{Sound: 7...Bf5 8.Qc1 and the queen has been chased twice. The trade keeps it simple.} ) )
7. axb3
{Take towards the centre. White gets the open a-file, a rook that is already on it, and a completely safe position. [%tags endgame] [%plan Play Nbd2, Bd3 and castle, then use the a-file with Ra1 to a7 ideas. The queens are off, so simply out-develop Black and squeeze.] [%line ...Nc6 first, with the queen trade] [%cal Ra1a7]}
) ( 3... Nf6
{Development first: the knight comes out before Black decides between ...Nc6, ...e6 and ...Qb6. [%weight 5]}
4. Nf3
{Develop and wait. Black has not committed to anything yet, and neither has White. [%tags development]}
4... e6
{Black settles for the solid setup. The sting of ...Qb6 fades once both sides are developed. [%weight 7]}
( 4... Nc6
{The knight comes out, and it is the ...Nc6 line by another order: the Slav course reaches this position with ...Nf6 first. [%weight 6]}
5. c3
{Hold d4 with the pawn, as in the ...Nc6 first line: the same position, so the same move, and 5...Qb6 6.Qb3 follows as it does there. [%tags development] [%plan The ...Nc6 first line from here: Qb3 against ...Qb6, Nbd2, Bd3 and O-O. One position, two move orders.] [%line ...Nf6 first, then ...Nc6]}
( 5. Nc3?!
{It blocks the c-pawn that holds d4, and 5...cxd4 6.exd4 Bg4 leaves the pawn on d4 with one defender.}
) ( 5. dxc5?! {It gives up the centre for a pawn Black takes back at once with 5...e6.} )
) 5. c3
{The usual triangle. This is the main line by transposition. [%tags development] [%plan Play Nbd2, Bd3 and castle, then Ne5 and f4. If Black tries ...Qb6 now, the answer is Qb3 offering the trade.] [%line ...c5 with ...Nf6 first]}
) ( 3... cxd4 {The immediate release, before either side has developed. [%weight 4]}
4. exd4
{Always this recapture. The e-file opens, the centre is strong and the bishop on f4 still has its diagonal. [%tags development] [%plan Follow with Nf3, c3, Bd3 and castle. Black has given up the tension for nothing, so simply develop and take the e-file.] [%line The immediate ...cxd4]}
( 4. Qxd4?
{The queen comes out early and Black gains time with ...Nc6, hitting it straight away.} )
) 4. Nc3
{The answer to learn cold: develop and ignore the threat. If Black takes on b2 the knight jumps to b5 and Black's queen is stranded while White develops with threats. [%tags vs-qb6 development] [%cal Gb1c3,Rb6b2]}
( 4. Qc1!?
{The safe alternative: defend b2 and lose nothing. It is passive, and the queen has to move again before the rooks connect.}
) ( 4. b3?!
{It defends the pawn but leaves holes on c3 and a3, and the bishop on f4 loses its retreat along the b8 to h2 diagonal.}
) ( 4. dxc5?!
{It holds the pawn but gives up the centre, and White keeps only a shade of an edge instead of the initiative Nc3 offers.}
) ( 4. Nd2?!
{The knight blocks the queen's defence of d4 and does not cover b5. Nc3 is the move that makes the pawn offer work.}
) 4... Qxb2 {The greedy move most club opponents cannot resist. [%weight 6]} ( 4... e6
{The sensible reply: cover c7 in advance and keep developing. [%weight 5]} 5. Nb5
{The same jump. Nc7 is threatened, so Black must spend a move on it while White has lost nothing. [%tags vs-qb6]}
( 5. Na4!?
{Hitting the queen is fine here too, but the knight on a4 has less to do than the one on b5.}
) ( 5. Rb1!?
{Solid and safe. It defends b2 but gives Black a free hand to finish developing.} )
5... Na6 {The only tidy way to cover c7. [%weight 7]} 6. Rb1
{Defend b2 and line the rook up against the queen. Offering the pawn again with c3 is just as good; the rook move needs no calculation. [%tags vs-qb6]}
( 6. c3!?
{Just as good, and it offers the b2 pawn on the same terms as before: after ...Qxb2 the rook still comes to b1 with tempo. Rb1 first is the simpler thing to remember.}
) ( 6. Nc7+?
{It loses a piece: ...Nxc7 takes the knight, and Bxc7 Qxc7 leaves White a bishop down as well.}
) 6... Nf6
{With the queenside holding for the moment, Black develops and hopes to castle before anything opens. [%weight 6]}
7. Nf3
{Finish developing. White has a normal position with an annoying knight on b5. [%tags development] [%plan Play c4 or c3 and Bd3, castle, and think about b4 to gain space with the rook already behind the pawn. Black's queen on b6 and knight on a6 are both awkwardly placed.] [%line ...Qb6 and then ...e6]}
) ( 4... Nc6
{Black declines the pawn and develops, keeping the pressure on d4 without committing the queen further. [%weight 5]}
5. Nxd5
{Not the knight jump here: count the defenders of d5 first. The queen went to b6 and the knight to c6, so nothing guards the pawn. Take it. [%tags vs-qb6 tactics] [%plan You are a clean pawn up with the better position. Meet ...Qxb2 with Nc3 or Rb1, get the king castled, and trade pieces when you can: an extra pawn is worth more once the queens come off.] [%line ...Qb6 and then ...Nc6] [%cal Gc3d5]}
( 5. Nb5?
{The jump from the other lines, played on autopilot. It gives back everything: the free pawn on d5 is the point of this position, not the fork threat.}
) ( 5. dxc5?!
{It wins nothing: the queen on b6 takes straight back on c5. Nxd5 is the capture that actually wins a pawn, because nothing is guarding d5.}
) ) 5. Nb5
{The point. White threatens Nc7 forking king and rook, and the queen on b2 is a long way from home. [%tags vs-qb6 tactics] [%plan Follow up with Rb1 and rapid development, and look for Nc7 or Nd6 tricks. You gave a pawn and got much more than a pawn back: Black has a queen with no good squares and no development at all. Keep making threats and do not hurry to win the material back.] [%line ...Qxb2, the pawn grab] [%cal Rb5c7]}
( 5. Nxd5!?
{The engine's first choice, about a pawn and a half stronger than the jump: it regains the pawn at once and still threatens Nc7. Kept out of the repertoire so there is one jump to remember, not two.}
) ( 5. Rb1?
{The rook hits the queen and leaves the knight hanging: 5...Qxc3+ takes it with check, so White has given a piece as well as the pawn.}
) ( 5. Na4?
{It hits the queen but gives up everything else. The knight on a4 does nothing, and after ...Qb4 or ...Nc6 Black is the one who is better.}
) *


[CourseId "london-white"]
[CourseTitle "The London System"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "One setup against almost everything, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-c5 vs-qb6 vs-kid vs-gruenfeld vs-bf5 vs-dutch"]
[ChapterId "ch3-kings-indian"]
[ChapterTitle "King's Indian setups"]
[DefaultTags "vs-kid"]

{# King's Indian setups

Black plays ...Nf6, ...g6 and ...d6, fianchettos the dark-squared bishop and castles. The pawn goes to d6, not d5. That one difference changes what White should do.

## What Black is doing

The bishop on g7 aims at d4 and the queenside. Black wants to break with ...e5, and if White closes the centre, to attack on the kingside with ...f5. Nothing is threatened early, so White has time to build.

## Two changes to your setup

The bishop goes to e2, not d3. The pawn on g6 already defends h7, so d3 aims at nothing; on e2 the bishop supports the centre and stays out of the way.

The c-pawn goes to c4, not c3. Black declined to put a pawn on d5, so the centre is there for the taking. This is the one chapter where White gets a full big centre, and it is the reward for Black playing ...d6.

## The h3 move earns its keep here

Play h3 early. When Black breaks with ...e5 and hits the bishop on f4, the answer is Bh2: same diagonal, no harm done, and the pawn on e5 becomes a target. Without h3 the bishop has to go to g3, where ...Nh5 can trade it off.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Putting the bishop on d3 out of habit, where the g6 pawn makes it useless. Playing c3 and settling for less than the position offers. Closing the centre with d5, which hands Black the standard ...f5 attack. Grabbing the pawn on e5 when it is defended twice.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6
{Black heads for a fianchetto. Watch the d-pawn: if it goes to d6 rather than d5, this is the chapter you are in. [%weight 8]}
( 2... d6
{Black keeps every option open, but the pawn on d6 already says this is not a ...d5 setup. [%weight 4]}
3. e3
{Nothing changes. Build the triangle and see what Black commits to. [%tags development]} (
3. Nc3?! {The c-pawn wants c4 against a d6 setup, so do not block it.} ) ( 3. c4!?
{Taking space at once works too. Playing e3 first means the bishop on f4 is never loose while the centre opens.}
) 3... g6
{Now the fianchetto is coming, and the d6 pawn has already said which chapter this is. [%weight 7]}
4. Nf3 {Develop the knight before choosing the bishop's square. [%tags development]}
4... Bg7
{The standard King's Indian picture: bishop on g7, pawn on d6, and the ...e5 break in the post. [%weight 8]}
5. Be2
{A fianchetto on the board means e2 for the bishop. [%tags bishop-placement] [%plan Castle, then h3 and c4. If Black breaks with ...e5, take and step back to h2; if with ...c5, hold with c3 and recapture towards the centre.] [%line The ...d6 move order]}
) 3. e3
{Build the triangle as always. Nothing Black has done changes the first few moves. [%tags development]}
( 3. Nc3?!
{It blocks the c-pawn, and against these setups the c-pawn wants to go to c4, not c3.} ) (
3. e4?!
{It drops a pawn. Nothing is guarding e4, so 3...Nxe4 simply takes it, and 4.Bd3 hits the knight without getting the pawn back. Grabbing the centre is a real idea in other openings; here e3 is what holds the structure together.}
) ( 3. h4?
{The pawn storm before any development. Black castles, opens the centre with ...c5 or ...e5, and White has nothing.}
) 3... Bg7
{The bishop takes the long diagonal, the point of Black's whole setup: it will press on d4 and the queenside from home. [%weight 9]}
( 3... d6 {The other move order into the same setups. [%weight 5]} 4. Nf3
{Develop and wait to see whether Black fianchettos. [%tags development]} ( 4. Bd3?!
{With ...g6 coming, the bishop belongs on e2. Wait one move and put it on the right square.}
) 4... Bg7
{The fianchetto completes. With the pawn on d6 rather than d5, this is the King's Indian shape proper. [%weight 8]}
5. Be2 {The fianchetto has arrived, so the bishop goes to e2. [%tags bishop-placement]}
5... O-O
{The king castles behind the fianchetto and Black is ready for ...Nbd7 and the ...e5 break. [%weight 8]}
6. h3
{The same position the main line reaches, so the same move: h3 first, keeping h2 free for the bishop. [%tags bishop-placement] [%plan Castle, then c4 for space. From here the chapter is one line: this is the main line reached by another move order.] [%line ...d6 before the bishop]}
( 6. O-O!?
{Safe, and the king is going there next move anyway. Playing h3 first means ...Nh5 never costs you the bishop pair.}
) ( 6. c3?!
{Black has played d6 rather than d5, so the pawn belongs on c4 taking the centre.} ) )
4. Nf3 {Develop and cover e5, which is the square Black wants to use. [%tags development]}
( 4. Be2!?
{The right square for the bishop, just a move early. Knight first keeps ...Nh5 and ...Bg4 in check.}
) ( 4. Bd3?!
{The pawn on g6 already covers h7, so the bishop is aiming at a defended square.} )
4... O-O
{Black castles early, as fianchetto players do: the king is safest behind the g6 and g7 wall. [%weight 8]}
5. Be2
{This is the bishop adaptation: e2, not d3. The g6 pawn covers h7, so d3 attacks nothing, while on e2 the bishop supports a later c4 and stays clear of ...Nh5. [%tags bishop-placement] [%csl Rg6] [%cal Gf1e2]}
( 5. Bd3?!
{The standard error against a fianchetto. The bishop stares at h7, which the pawn on g6 already defends, and it blocks the d-file.}
) ( 5. c4!?
{Also good, and the pawn is going there anyway. Getting the king safe first costs nothing.}
) 5... d6
{The move that defines this chapter. With d6 rather than d5, Black leaves the centre to White for now. [%weight 8]}
( 5... c5 {Hitting the centre from the other side. [%weight 5]} 6. c3
{Here the pawn does go to c3. Black has hit d4 directly, so holding the centre comes before taking space. [%tags development]}
( 6. dxc5?! {Black recaptures with ...Qa5+ or ...dxc5 and has a free game.} ) 6... cxd4
{Black releases the tension, hoping d4 becomes a target. It also opens the e-file for White, so the trade cuts both ways. [%weight 6]}
7. exd4
{Take towards the centre and open the e-file. The bishop on f4 keeps its diagonal. [%tags development] [%plan Play O-O, Nbd2 and Re1. The pawn on d4 needs watching, but Black's bishop on g7 is biting on granite while it stands there.] [%line ...c5 instead of ...d6]}
) 6. h3
{The small move that matters. It opens h2 for the bishop, so a later ...Nh5 or ...e5 cannot chase it off the good diagonal. [%tags bishop-placement] [%cal Gf4h2]}
( 6. O-O!?
{Perfectly safe. Playing h3 first means the bishop never has to go to g3, where ...Nh5 can trade it off.}
) ( 6. Nc3?!
{The c-pawn wants c4 here, because Black has not contested the centre with ...d5.} ) (
6. Ng5? {The knight has nothing to attack and Black plays ...h6, winning time.} )
6... Nbd7 {Black prepares ...e5, the standard King's Indian break. [%weight 6]} 7. O-O
{King safety first, and now the rook on f1 is behind the f-pawn if the position ever opens. [%tags king-safety]}
( 7. c4!?
{The move White wants, one move early. Castling first means ...e5 can never come with a tactic against e1.}
) ( 7. e4?!
{It blocks the bishop's own diagonal and gives Black the e5 square with tempo.} ) 7... Re8
{Backing up the ...e5 break. [%weight 5]} 8. c4
{Now take the space. Black played d6 rather than d5, so White is allowed a full centre and the bishop on e2 supports it. [%tags c4-break] [%cal Gc2c4]}
( 8. c3?!
{Too modest. The whole point of Black's d6 is that c4 is available, and c3 wastes it. It does threaten one thing, which a prepared Black player knows: 8...e5 9.dxe5 dxe5 10.Nxe5 wins the pawn, because 10...Nxe5 11.Qxd8 Rxd8 12.Bxe5 takes it back with the queens off, so the King's Indian course holds ...e5 back after c3.}
) ( 8. e4?
{Count the defenders of e4 before pushing: there are none. Black replies ...Nxe4, and if the knight is chased, ...e5 breaks the centre open anyway.}
) 8... e5
{The break Black has been building towards. It also hits the bishop on f4. [%weight 7]}
9. dxe5
{Take. The pawn on e5 that comes back is easier to play against than a big Black centre. [%tags c4-break]}
( 9. d5?
{It forgets what ...e5 attacked. The pawn hits f4 as well as d4, so 9...exf4 10.exf4 leaves White a bishop down for a pawn.}
) ( 9. Bh2!?
{Keeping the tension is reasonable, but taking gives White a clear target on e5.} )
9... dxe5
{Black recaptures and the centre settles: the e5 pawn is Black's stake in the game, and also White's target. [%weight 8]}
10. Bh2
{Here is the payoff for h3. The bishop steps back to h2, still on the long diagonal, and Black has gained nothing by pushing the pawn. [%tags bishop-placement dark-squares] [%cal Bh2b8]}
( 10. Bxe5?
{The pawn is defended twice, by the knight on d7 and the rook on e8. After the trades White has given a bishop for a pawn.}
) ( 10. Bg3!? {It also keeps the bishop, but on h2 it can never be hit by ...Nh5.} ) (
10. Nxe5!?
{Taking is sound, but know the trap that follows: after ...Nxe5, do NOT recapture with the bishop, because the d-file is open and ...Qxd1 comes first. Bh2 keeps the position simple and the bishop on its diagonal.}
) 10... Ne4
{The knight jumps to e4, the square this setup leaves loose: no pawn reaches it with c4 and e3 played, and the bishop on e2 does not see it. This is what the King's Indian course plays here, and it asks a question the bishop on h2 does not answer. [%weight 6]}
11. Qc2
{Hit the knight with the queen. It has to be held by ...f5 or traded by ...Nec5, and either way the pawn on e5 stays the target the bishop on h2 is aiming at. The engine's choice at 2500 ms, a little ahead of the knight moves. [%tags development dark-squares] [%plan Play Nc3, Qc2 and the rooks to d1 and e1. Black's pawn on e5 is a target and the dark-squared bishop on h2 is aiming straight through it. Do not rush: this is a slow squeeze, not an attack.] [%line The full main line, with ...e5]}
( 11. Bxe5?
{The pawn is still defended twice: 11...Nxe5 12.Nxe5 Rxe5 and a bishop has gone for a pawn.}
) ( 11. Nfd2!?
{Sound: 11...Nxd2 12.Qxd2 Nc5 and the queens are likely to come off level. The queen move keeps them on and keeps the target.}
) ( 11. Nbd2!?
{Sound: 11...Nxd2 12.Qxd2 and it is level. The queen first makes Black hold the knight before it is traded.}
) *


[CourseId "london-white"]
[CourseTitle "The London System"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "One setup against almost everything, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-c5 vs-qb6 vs-kid vs-gruenfeld vs-bf5 vs-dutch"]
[ChapterId "ch4-gruenfeld"]
[ChapterTitle "Grunfeld setups"]
[DefaultTags "vs-gruenfeld"]

{# Grunfeld setups

Black fianchettos with ...g6 and also puts a pawn on d5. It looks like the last chapter and plays quite differently, so check the d-pawn before you choose a plan.

## The one difference that matters

In the King's Indian setups Black plays ...d6 and White answers with c4, taking a big centre. Here Black has a pawn on d5, so c4 achieves nothing and costs something: it opens lines for the bishop on g7, which is the piece you want to keep looking at your solid pawn on d4.

So the pawn goes to c3 and stays there. This is the slow chapter.

## Your setup

Bf4, e3, Nf3, Be2, castle, c3, h3, Nbd2, then Ne5 and f4. The bishop goes to e2 for the same reason as the last chapter: the pawn on g6 covers h7, so d3 aims at a defended square.

The h3 move is worth playing early. Black's ...Nh5 is the standard nuisance in these positions, and Bh2 is a much better answer than Bg3.

## Where the play comes from

White's chances are the e5 square and the kingside. Black's are the long diagonal and the queenside. Keep the centre closed and you keep the better half of that bargain: the bishop on g7 spends the game staring at a pawn on d4 that has two defenders.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Playing c4 out of habit from the King's Indian chapter and opening the diagonal. Taking on c5, which does the same thing for free. Putting the bishop on d3 where the g6 pawn makes it pointless. Jumping to e5 before c3, d4 and the knight on d2 are there to support it.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 g6
{A fianchetto is coming. Whether this is chapter 3 or chapter 4 depends on the d-pawn. [%weight 8]}
( 2... d5
{The other route in: Black takes the centre first and fianchettos afterwards. [%weight 6]}
3. e3 {The usual triangle. Nothing here needs a decision yet. [%tags development]} (
3. Nc3?! {It blocks the c-pawn, which is the pawn holding your centre together.} ) 3... g6
{Now the fianchetto arrives, and with a pawn already on d5 this is a chapter 4 position. [%weight 6]}
4. Nf3
{Develop. The bishop's square is settled the moment the g-pawn moves. [%tags development]}
( 4. Bd3?! {The pawn on g6 has just taken away the point of that square.} ) ( 4. c4?!
{With a Black pawn on d5 and a bishop coming to g7, opening the centre helps Black more than White.}
) 4... Bg7
{The familiar picture: g7 bishop, d5 pawn, and a long game of plans ahead. [%weight 8]}
5. Be2 {Bishop to e2, and the rest follows itself. [%tags bishop-placement]} 5... O-O
{Black castles, and the third route in arrives at the same position as the others. [%weight 8]}
6. O-O
{Castle and take stock. White has the easier position to play because every piece has a known square. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Play c3, h3 and Nbd2, then Ne5 with f4. If Black breaks with ...c5, hold with c3 and never take on c5 while that bishop sits on g7.] [%line The ...d5 move order]}
) 3. e3 {Build the triangle and wait for Black to commit the d-pawn. [%tags development]}
( 3. Nc3?! {It blocks the c-pawn before you know whether the pawn wants c3 or c4.} ) (
3. c4!?
{A real alternative that grabs space, but it becomes a different opening once Black replies ...d5.}
) 3... d5
{This is the move that puts you in this chapter rather than the last one. Black has a pawn in the centre, so c4 is no longer a free lunch. [%weight 7]}
( 3... Bg7 {Black develops first and keeps the d-pawn's choice open. [%weight 6]} 4. Nf3
{Develop and wait. The bishop's square is already decided by the pawn on g6. [%tags development]}
( 4. Bd3?!
{There is a pawn on g6. The bishop belongs on e2 whatever Black does with the d-pawn.} )
4... d5
{Now the d-pawn commits to d5, and the chapter is decided: the slow plan, not the c4 grab. [%weight 7]}
5. Be2
{A fianchetto plus a pawn on d5 means the slow plan: build, castle, and aim for Ne5. [%tags bishop-placement]}
5... O-O
{Black castles behind the fianchetto, solid as ever. A slow manoeuvring game is coming. [%weight 8]}
6. O-O
{The setup is complete and nothing can be taken from White. [%tags king-safety] [%plan Play c3 and h3, then Nbd2 and Ne5 with f4 behind it. Do not open the centre while Black's bishop is on g7 looking through it.] [%line ...Bg7 before ...d5]}
) 4. Nf3
{Develop and cover e5. With ...d5 played, White builds rather than grabs. [%tags development]}
( 4. c4?!
{It opens the position for the bishop on g7 and hands Black the ...dxc4 and ...c5 counterplay. Save c4 for the setups where Black plays d6.}
) ( 4. Bd3?! {The pawn on g6 covers h7, so the bishop has nothing to aim at from d3.} )
4... Bg7
{The bishop takes the long diagonal, where it will stare at White's well defended d4 pawn for a long time. [%weight 9]}
5. Be2
{Bishop to e2 against any fianchetto. It is out of the way, it supports the centre, and ...Nh5 cannot hit it. [%tags bishop-placement] [%csl Rg6] [%cal Gf1e2]}
( 5. Bd3?! {The habit from the ...e6 chapters, applied where it does not belong.} ) (
5. c3!? {The pawn is going to c3 sooner or later, so this only changes the order.} )
5... O-O
{Black castles. The setup is comfortable and solid, and the middlegame will be decided by the plans, not the opening. [%weight 8]}
6. O-O
{Both kings safe. Now White finishes the triangle and looks for the e5 outpost. [%tags king-safety]}
( 6. Ne5?!
{The knight arrives before the support does, and Black chases it with ...Nfd7 or ...c5.} )
( 6. h4?
{An attack with the king still in the centre, against a position with no weaknesses. Black opens the centre and White is worse.}
) 6... c5 {The standard break at the base of the triangle. [%weight 6]} 7. c3
{Hold d4. Black cannot win the pawn, and a solid centre is what keeps the bishop on g7 quiet. [%tags development] [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3]}
( 7. dxc5?!
{It opens the long diagonal straight at your queenside. The bishop on g7 has been waiting for this since move two.}
) ( 7. c4?! {The same problem: opening lines helps the fianchettoed bishop, not you.} )
7... Nc6
{The knight adds to the pressure on d4, the natural way to make the g7 bishop count. [%weight 7]}
8. h3
{The escape square again. With a pawn on h3 the bishop answers ...Nh5 with Bh2 and keeps its diagonal. [%tags bishop-placement] [%cal Gf4h2]}
( 8. Nbd2!?
{The knight is going there anyway. Slipping in h3 first means ...Nh5 and ...Bg4 never cost you anything.}
) ( 8. Ne5?!
{Too early: the knight has no support yet, so Black trades it off with ...Nxe5 on comfortable terms.}
) ( 8. dxc5?!
{It gives up the centre and opens the long diagonal for the bishop on g7, which is the piece you least want to help.}
) 8... b6 {Black finishes developing on the queenside. [%weight 5]} ( 8... Nh5
{The harassment try, which the last move already answered. [%weight 4]} 9. Bh2
{Straight back to h2. The bishop keeps the long diagonal, the knight on h5 is offside, and White has lost nothing. [%tags bishop-placement] [%plan Play Nbd2 and Ne5, and remember Black's knight on h5 is not defending anything. If it comes back to f6 you have gained two free moves.] [%line Black's ...Nh5 trade] [%cal Bh2b8]}
( 9. Bg3?
{Now ...Nxg3 trades off your best piece, which is the one thing h3 was played to prevent. Two moves wasted and the bishop gone.}
) ( 9. Be5!? {Also possible, but on e5 the bishop can be challenged by ...Nc6 or ...f6.} )
) 9. Nbd2
{The queen's knight to d2, as ever, so the pawn on c3 keeps holding d4. [%tags development]}
( 9. Ne5!? {Fine, but the knight is happier once the other one is on d2 to back it up.} )
( 9. c4?!
{Here it helps Black: the centre opens and the bishop on g7 gets the long diagonal it has been waiting for.}
) 9... Bb7
{The second bishop fianchettos too. Black's pressure runs along both long diagonals, all of it pointed at the centre White is holding. [%weight 7]}
10. Ne5
{The outpost, supported by c3, d4 and the knight on d2. This is as good as the London gets against a fianchetto. [%tags kingside-attack] [%plan Play f4 to weld the knight in place, then Qf3 or Rf3 and swing at the king. Keep an eye on ...cxd4 and ...Nxe5: if the knight is traded, recapture with a pawn so the outpost stays occupied.] [%line The full main line, with ...b6] [%csl Ge5]}
( 10. Qb3!?
{Hitting d5 is a real idea, but the knight jump is simpler and improves your worst-placed piece.}
) ( 10. g4?
{A pawn storm in front of your own castled king, with the centre still open and a bishop on b7 aiming at it.}
) *


