{# Harassment tries

Some opponents will not build a setup against you. They come straight at the bishop on f4 instead, or grab a square in the centre, hoping you have only memorised the quiet lines. There are three of these and each has one answer.

## ...Nh5, hitting the bishop

Answer Bg3 every time. If Black takes with ...Nxg3, recapture with the h-pawn. That opens the h-file straight at the black king and your rook is already on it. You have swapped a bishop for a knight and been paid an attacking file for the trouble. Never recapture with the f-pawn: it wrecks your kingside and shuts in the rook.

If Black leaves the knight sitting on h5, ignore it and develop. A knight on the rim defends nothing.

## ...Bf5, copying your idea

Black gets the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain, which is a good move. Answer Bd3, offering the trade. If Black takes, recapture with the queen: it lands on the diagonal pointing at h7. If Black avoids it with ...Bg6, the bishop ends up staring at your own bishop and doing very little, so leave it there rather than trading on your opponent's terms.

## ...Ne4, the lunge

A knight on e4 looks frightening and is supported by nothing. Play Nd2, attacking it. If Black trades, take back with the queen and get on with the game a pair of pieces lighter and a tempo up.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Recapturing on g3 with the f-pawn. Retreating the bishop to d2, which throws away the whole point of the opening. Chasing pieces with pawns like g4 or f3, which weakens the king. Bringing the queen out early to defend the bishop, where it gets chased around and White loses more time than the bishop was worth.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 Nf6
{The natural developing move. From here Black can play a normal game or start poking at the bishop. [%weight 8]}
( 2... Bf5
{Black copies White's idea and gets the bishop outside the pawn chain before playing ...e6. [%weight 5]}
3. e3
{Build the triangle. Black's bishop on f5 is well placed, so White's answer is to offer to trade it. [%tags vs-bf5 development]}
( 3. c4!?
{A real try: it hits d5 and the bishop has left b7 undefended. It is also a different opening, so it is out of this repertoire.}
) ( 3. Nf3!?
{Fine and it usually transposes. Playing e3 first means Bd3 comes next with no interruption.}
) ( 3. Nc3?!
{It blocks the c-pawn, and c3 is the square that holds d4 once Black plays ...c5.} )
3... e6
{Black completes the little wall. With the bishop already outside, ...e6 costs nothing here, which is the whole point of playing ...Bf5 first. [%weight 7]}
4. Bd3
{Offer the trade. Black's bishop on f5 is the good one, so swapping it is a small win, and if Black avoids the trade the bishop ends up on g6 doing less. [%tags vs-bf5 bishop-placement] [%cal Gd3f5]}
( 4. Nf3!? {Sound, but Bd3 asks Black a question straight away and nothing else does.} ) (
4. Be2?! {Passive. There is no pawn on g6 here, so the bishop belongs on the d3 diagonal.}
) ( 4. Bb5+?!
{A check with nothing behind it. Black blocks with ...c6 or ...Nc6 and White has lost a move.}
) 4... Bg6
{Black keeps the bishop, but on g6 it is biting on White's own bishop and does much less. [%weight 5]}
( 4... Bxd3 {Black takes, which is what White was hoping for. [%weight 6]} 5. Qxd3
{Recapture with the queen, not the pawn. The queen lands on the b1 to h7 diagonal, eyeing h7. [%tags vs-bf5 kingside-attack] [%cal Bd3h7]}
( 5. cxd3?
{It doubles the pawns, gives up the good diagonal and takes away the c3 square the pawn needs. There is no upside.}
) 5... Nf6
{Black develops and keeps the position simple: the light-squared bishops are gone and the plans are clear. [%weight 7]}
6. Nf3 {Develop and head for e5. White has the better bishop left on the board.} (
6. Qb5+?!
{A check that helps Black develop with ...Nbd7 or ...c6, and the queen has to come back.}
) 6... Bd6
{The offer to trade the dark-squared bishops as well: Black wants a simple, level game. [%weight 5]}
7. Bg3 {The same rule as ever: keep the bishop. [%tags bishop-placement]} 7... O-O
{Black castles into a quiet position. The play from here is about the e5 square and the queenside pawns. [%weight 7]}
8. Nbd2
{Finish developing and castle next. White is comfortable with no weaknesses at all. [%plan Castle, play c3, then Ne5 and f4. The queen on d3 is already pointing at h7, so a knight on e5 and a rook lift is often enough to create real threats.] [%line ...Bf5 and the d3 trade]}
) 5. Nf3
{Carry on developing. The bishop on g6 is not going anywhere, and it will end up traded on White's terms.}
( 5. Bxg6!?
{Trading is fine and you will do it soon anyway. Right now the queen can still recapture on d3, so there is no hurry.}
) ( 5. Ne2?! {The knight blocks the bishop and heads nowhere useful.} ) 5... Nf6
{Development continues around the bishop stand-off on the b1 to h7 diagonal. [%weight 8]}
6. O-O
{King safety first. The pieces are already on their squares, so there is nothing to hurry about. [%tags king-safety]}
( 6. Ne5?!
{Too early. Black answers ...Nbd7 or ...Nfd7, trades, and White has to recapture with the king still in the middle.}
) ( 6. h4?
{An attack before castling, against a position with no weakness. Black opens the centre with ...c5 and the king on e1 is the one in danger.}
) 6... Bd6
{The usual trade offer, now with both light-squared bishops facing each other as well. [%weight 5]}
7. Bg3
{Keep the bishop, as always. If Black takes on g3 the h-file opens towards the black king. [%tags bishop-placement h-file]}
( 7. Bxd6?!
{It gives Black a free developing move with ...Qxd6 and removes White's best piece.} ) (
7. Be5!?
{Playable, and it does stop ...Bxf4, but the bishop can be challenged there and Bg3 keeps things simple.}
) 7... O-O
{Both sides castled, both dark-squared bishops eye to eye: a quiet middlegame of plans is coming. [%weight 7]}
8. Nbd2 {The last minor piece comes out, keeping c3 free for the pawn.} ( 8. Nc3?!
{It takes the square the c-pawn needs to hold d4.} ) 8... c5
{The familiar strike at the base of White's centre. [%weight 6]} 9. Bxg6
{Trade now, and know why. With a knight on d2 and the c-pawn about to leave c2, nothing could recapture on d3: the queen road is blocked and the pawn has gone. Take first and the problem never arises. [%tags vs-bf5 bishop-placement] [%cal Gd3g6,Rg6d3]}
( 9. c3?
{This is the trap. After ...Bxd3 there is no way to take back: the knight on d2 blocks the queen and the c-pawn has just left c2. A whole bishop for nothing.}
) ( 9. dxc5?!
{It keeps the bishops on and leaves the same problem one move later. Trade on g6 first.} )
9... hxg6
{Black takes back towards the centre, the tidy recapture: the g6 pawn now covers f5 and h5. [%weight 8]}
10. c3
{Now the triangle is safe to finish. Black has rebuilt towards the centre and opened the h-file for a rook, so keep the king where it is and play in the middle. [%plan Play Qc2 or Re1, then Ne5 with f4 behind it. The black king has one fewer pawn in front of it, but the open file belongs to Black, so build before you attack.] [%line The early ...Bf5]}
) 3. e3
{The usual move. It also opens the queen road to h5, which matters the moment Black reaches for ...Nh5.}
( 3. Nc3?! {It blocks the c-pawn, which is the pawn that holds d4.} ) ( 3. h3!?
{Useful in itself, and it takes ...Bg4 and some ...Nh5 lines out of the game. Development first is simpler to remember.}
) 3... Nh5 {The poke, played a move too early. [%weight 3]} ( 3... e6
{The normal move. Now the poke at the bishop becomes a real question. [%weight 7]} 4. Nf3
{Develop, and notice what this move does besides developing: the knight on f3 blocks the queen road to h5, so from here on ...Nh5 has to be answered properly.}
( 4. Bd3!? {Fine, but the knight first means ...Nh5 never comes with the bishop loose.} )
( 4. h3!? {Useful in itself and it takes ...Bg4 away. Develop first.} ) 4... Nh5
{The poke, now that the queen cannot simply take. [%weight 5]} 5. Bg3
{Step back and let Black decide. Qxh5 is not on offer this time, because the knight on f3 is standing in the queen road. Taking on g3 opens the h-file for White, which is a bargain at the price of one bishop. [%tags bishop-placement h-file]}
( 5. Be5!?
{Also good and it avoids the trade, but Bg3 is one answer that works every time and needs no thought.}
) ( 5. Bg5?!
{The bishop cannot be chased off it: 5...h6 6.Bxd8 costs Black the queen for a bishop, because g5 looks straight at d8 through the empty f6 square. The objection is positional. 5.Bg3 keeps the bishop on the diagonal the system is built on, and invites Nxg3, when hxg3 opens the h-file.}
) 5... Nxg3 {Black takes, and gives White exactly what the h-file is worth. [%weight 6]} (
5... Be7 {Black leaves the knight on h5, where it is offside. [%weight 5]} 6. Bd3
{Ignore it and carry on. A knight on the rim defends nothing, so White is effectively a piece up where the game is. [%tags bishop-placement] [%plan Play c3, Nbd2 and castle. If the knight comes back to f6 you have gained two free moves; if it takes on g3, recapture with the h-pawn and take the file.] [%line ...Nh5 and then ...Be7]}
( 6. Be2!?
{It hits the knight, and Black just plays ...Nxg3 or ...Nf6. The bishop is worth more on the d3 diagonal.}
) ( 6. h3?!
{Useful in itself, and it lets Black settle: developing first is worth about a pawn and a half here, because the knight on the rim has to be answered before it is helped.}
) ) 6. hxg3
{Take towards the centre. The h-file is open at the black king, the g3 pawn covers f4 and h4, and White has a rook on the file. [%tags h-file kingside-attack] [%cal Rh1h8]}
( 6. fxg3?
{It wrecks the kingside, shuts in the rook on f1 and gives up the open file. Always take with the h-pawn.}
) 6... c5
{Black gets on with the standard counterplay against d4, the right idea even after the trade. [%weight 6]}
7. c3
{Hold the centre before starting anything. An attack only works if the middle of the board is stable.}
7... Nc6 {Develop and press on d4, Black's normal queenside play. [%weight 7]} 8. Bd3
{The bishop takes the diagonal towards h7, and White has both the open h-file and the standard London attack. [%tags bishop-placement kingside-attack] [%plan Castle short or leave the king in the centre and play Qc2, Ne5 and g4. With the h-file open, doubling rooks on it is usually stronger than anything Black has on the queenside.] [%line ...Nh5 and the g3 trade]}
) ( 3... Ne4
{The lunge. The knight looks impressive on e4 but nothing supports it. [%weight 4]} 4. Nd2
{Challenge it at once. The knight on d2 attacks e4, and if Black trades, White recaptures with the queen and gains time. [%cal Rd2e4]}
( 4. Nc3?!
{It also hits the knight, but it blocks the c-pawn and Black can answer ...Nxc3 leaving White with doubled pawns.}
) ( 4. f3?!
{It chases the knight but weakens e3 and the king's diagonal for the rest of the game.} )
( 4. Nf3!?
{Developing and ignoring the knight is fine, but challenging it straight away is the move that gains time.}
) 4... Nxd2
{Black trades the adventurous knight off rather than spend another move retreating it. [%weight 6]}
5. Qxd2
{Take with the queen. It keeps f3 free for the other knight and the queen is well placed on d2 behind the pawn chain.}
( 5. Kxd2?
{The king walks into the middle of the board with queens still on. There is never a reason for this here.}
) 5... e6
{Back to sensible development: open the bishop's diagonal and prepare to castle. [%weight 7]}
6. Nf3
{Develop the remaining knight. White has traded a pair of pieces and kept a comfortable position.}
( 6. Bd3!? {Also fine. The knight first keeps ...Nc6 and ...e5 ideas under control.} )
6... c5
{The standard strike at d4. Nothing about the early trade changed Black's right plan. [%weight 6]}
7. c3 {Hold d4 and the position is completely solid.} 7... Nc6
{The knight backs the c5 pawn and the queenside play is properly under way. [%weight 7]}
8. Bd3
{The setup is complete, one pair of knights lighter and with the trade having cost Black time. [%tags bishop-placement] [%plan Castle, then Ne5 and f4 as usual. With fewer pieces on the board, keep the bishop pair and remember the endgame is pleasant for White here.] [%line The ...Ne4 lunge]}
) 4. Qxh5
{Count before you retreat. The bishop is attacked, but the knight on h5 is defended by nothing, and with the pawn on e3 the queen has a clear road to it. Take the piece. [%tags tactics] [%plan You are a piece up. Play Nf3 and Be2, castle, and trade whenever you can. The rule to take away: before you move an attacked piece, check whether the attacker is defended.] [%line The immediate ...Nh5] [%cal Gd1h5]}
( 4. Bg3?
{The answer from the proper move order, played without looking. It costs a whole piece: the knight on h5 was free.}
) ( 4. Be5? {It saves the bishop and forgets the knight. Take on h5 instead.} ) ( 4. Bg5?
{The same oversight. Nothing defends h5, so nothing else matters here.} ) *


[CourseId "london-white"]
[CourseTitle "The London System"]
[StudentColour "white"]
[Tagline "One setup against almost everything, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-c5 vs-qb6 vs-kid vs-gruenfeld vs-bf5 vs-dutch"]
[ChapterId "ch6-everything-else"]
[ChapterTitle "Everything else"]
[DefaultTags "development"]

{# Everything else

The odds and ends: everything Black can play on move one that the earlier chapters do not cover. One safe recipe each, and none of them needs much memorising.

## The Dutch, 1...f5

The one that changes something. Look at the b1 to h7 diagonal: the pawn on f5 sits right across it, so the bishop on d3 would be staring at its own dead end. The bishop goes to e2 instead.

After that it is the King's Indian recipe. Black has no pawn on d5, so take the space with c4, castle early because Black's whole plan is a kingside attack, and meet ...Ne4 by challenging it with Nc3.

## 1...c5, and the one exception to bishop first

This is the only line in the repertoire where e3 comes before Bf4, and it is worth knowing why rather than remembering it. Black has played ...c5 without ...d5, so the c7 square is already empty. That makes ...Qb6 stronger than usual, and it also means ...cxd4 comes without the loss of time that normally makes it harmless.

So play 2.e3, take back on d4 with the e-pawn, and bring the bishop out next move with a solid centre already behind it. One move later, and nothing lost.

## 1...b6, 1...e6, 1...g6

Nothing changes. Bishop out, triangle, knight, castle. With ...b6 or ...e6 the bishop goes to d3 as usual, because the h7 diagonal is clear. With ...g6, wait one move: if Black follows with ...d6 you are in chapter 3, and if ...d5, chapter 4.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Putting the bishop on d3 against the Dutch, where a pawn on f5 blocks it. Playing 2.Bf4 against 1...c5 out of habit and having to solve problems that e3 would have prevented. Reaching for a different opening, an early e4 or c4, because the position looks unfamiliar. Starting a kingside attack before castling, which the Dutch punishes harder than anything else.}
1. d4 f5
{The Dutch. Black grabs e4 and plans a kingside attack, at the cost of loosening the king. [%weight 4]}
( 1... c5
{Straight at d4 before White has built anything. This is the one place the bishop does not come out first. [%weight 5]}
2. e3
{The exception to the rule, and worth knowing why. After 2.Bf4 Black has ...Qb6 hitting b2 and d4 at once, and also ...cxd4 winning the pawn back on good terms, because the c7 square is already empty. Hold the centre first and the bishop comes out next move. [%tags vs-c5 development] [%csl Gd4]}
( 2. Bf4?!
{The usual move, but here ...cxd4 is awkward: taking back with the queen invites ...Nc6 with tempo, and ...Qb6 comes with an extra threat because c7 is free.}
) ( 2. dxc5?!
{It hands Black the centre and a free developing move with ...e6 and ...Bxc5.} ) ( 2. d5!?
{A real alternative that turns the game into a Benoni. It is sound and it is a different opening.}
) 2... cxd4
{Black takes at once: the point of 1...c5 is to trade off White's centre pawn early. [%weight 6]}
3. exd4
{Take towards the centre. Now the bishop can come to f4 with the pawn on d4 already solid.}
( 3. Qxd4? {The queen comes out early and ...Nc6 chases it straight away.} ) 3... d5
{Black stakes the centre in return, and the position becomes a quiet, symmetrical one. [%weight 6]}
4. Bf4
{Now the bishop comes out, one move later than usual and none the worse for it. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 4. c4!? {It hits d5 and is perfectly sound, but it leaves this repertoire behind.} )
4... Nf6
{Normal development in a normal position: the sting of 1...c5 has gone. [%weight 8]}
5. Nf3
{Back to the normal plan: knight, bishop, castle. [%plan Play Bd3 or Be2, castle, and hold d4 with c3. The pawn on d4 is isolated in some lines, so keep pieces on it and remember the e5 square is yours.] [%line The ...c5 first move]}
) ( 1... b6
{Black fianchettos on the queenside and leaves the centre alone for now. [%weight 4]}
2. Bf4
{Nothing here needs a new idea. The bishop comes out and the setup follows. [%tags bishop-placement]}
( 2. e4!?
{Taking the whole centre is good chess and a well known answer to ...b6. It is also not the London.}
) ( 2. Nc3?! {The c-pawn wants its square, and Black has done nothing to make you hurry.}
) 2... Bb7
{The bishop goes straight to the long diagonal, the whole idea of 1...b6: pressure on e4 and the centre from the side. [%weight 8]}
3. e3
{Build the triangle. The bishop on b7 is aiming at g2 and a pawn on e3 does not care.}
3... e6
{Black opens the other bishop's diagonal and keeps the centre flexible. [%weight 7]}
4. Nf3
{Develop, and look at what it stops: the knight on f3 blocks the b7 bishop road to g2. Until it is there, the g2 pawn is only defended by the bishop on f1.}
( 4. Bd3?
{It hangs g2. The bishop on f1 was the only thing defending it, and the bishop on b7 is looking straight down that diagonal: ...Bxg2 wins a pawn and the rook is next. Play Nf3 first and the diagonal is shut.}
) 4... Nf6
{Development continues. Black delays the d-pawn, keeping both ...d5 and ...d6 in reserve. [%weight 8]}
5. Bd3
{Here d3 is right: the diagonal to h7 is clear and Black will castle into it. [%tags bishop-placement] [%cal Bd3h7]}
( 5. Be2?!
{Passive. There is no pawn on g6 or f5, so the bishop should take the diagonal that matters.}
) 5... Be7
{The quiet square again: Black finishes developing and will castle next. [%weight 7]}
6. Nbd2
{The last minor piece, keeping c3 free for the pawn. [%plan Castle, play c3, then Ne5 and f4 as usual. Black's bishop on b7 is looking at a pawn on d5 that is not there yet, so it is the quieter piece.] [%line The ...b6 fianchetto]}
) ( 1... g6 {A fianchetto is coming, so the answer depends on the d-pawn. [%weight 5]}
2. Bf4 {Bishop out, and the d-pawn tells you the rest. [%tags bishop-placement]} ( 2. e4!?
{Taking the centre is good and it is not this repertoire.} ) ( 2. h4?
{The pawn lunge against a fianchetto, before any development. Black castles and opens the centre.}
) 2... Bg7
{The fianchetto completes. Which chapter this becomes still depends on the d-pawn. [%weight 8]}
3. e3
{Build the triangle and wait one more move. [%plan If Black plays ...d6, you are in chapter 3: bishop to e2 and take the centre with c4. If ...d5, you are in chapter 4: bishop to e2 and hold with c3.] [%line The ...g6 fianchetto]}
) ( 1... e6
{A waiting move. It usually becomes a position from chapter 1, or a Dutch if ...f5 follows. [%weight 4]}
2. Bf4 {Bishop out first, then see what Black has in mind. [%tags bishop-placement]} (
2. e4!? {This is a French Defence, which is fine chess and a whole other opening.} )
2... Nf6
{Black develops flexibly. This can still become a classical ...d5 game, a Dutch with ...f5, or a queenside fianchetto. [%weight 7]}
3. e3
{The triangle again. Black is yet to say anything about the centre. [%plan If Black plays ...d5 you are in chapter 1. If ...f5, you are in the Dutch above and the bishop goes to e2. If ...b6, play Bd3 and carry on.] [%line The ...e6 move order]}
) 2. Bf4
{The same first move as ever. The bishop is outside the pawn chain before anything else happens. [%tags vs-dutch bishop-placement]}
( 2. Bg5!?
{A real anti-Dutch try, hitting the squares Black has weakened. It is a different opening to learn, and this repertoire does not need one.}
) ( 2. e4?!
{The gambit is playable but sharp, and it throws away the setup you actually know.} ) (
2. g4? {Attacking with the pawn in front of your own king before a single piece is out.} )
2... Nf6
{The knight develops and holds the e4 square, which the whole Dutch setup is built around. [%weight 8]}
3. e3 {Build the triangle. Nothing about the Dutch changes the first three moves.} 3... e6
{Black opens the bishop's diagonal and gets ready to castle: the classical Dutch shape. [%weight 7]}
4. Nf3 {Develop and cover e5 and g5, the squares Black would like for a knight.} ( 4. Bd3?
{Look at the diagonal: the pawn on f5 sits right across it. The bishop would be aiming at its own dead end.}
) ( 4. Nc3?! {It blocks the c-pawn, and against the Dutch the pawn wants c4.} ) 4... Be7
{The modest square, as usual in the classical Dutch: castle first, expand later. [%weight 7]}
5. Be2
{Here the bishop goes to e2 for a different reason than usual: the pawn on f5 blocks the d3 diagonal completely, so d3 would be a wasted square. [%tags bishop-placement] [%csl Rf5] [%cal Gf1e2]}
( 5. Bd3?!
{The habit from the ...d5 chapters. The pawn on f5 makes that diagonal useless while it stands there.}
) ( 5. c4!?
{Also good and the pawn is going there. Getting the pieces out first keeps things simple.}
) 5... O-O
{Black castles, completing the setup. The plan is ...d6, ...Qe8 and a slow kingside build. [%weight 8]}
6. O-O
{Castle before doing anything else. Black's whole plan is a kingside attack, so the king wants to be tucked away and the rook on f1 wants to be behind the f-pawn. [%tags king-safety]}
( 6. Ne5?!
{Too early, and with the king in the centre Black gets ...Nc6 and ...d6 with tempo.} ) (
6. h3!?
{Useful, but the Dutch does not usually come with ...Nh5, so castling matters more here.}
) 6... d6 {Black prepares ...e5, the break that gives the Dutch its play. [%weight 7]}
7. c4
{Black has no pawn on d5, so take the space, exactly as against the King's Indian setups. [%tags c4-break] [%cal Gc2c4]}
( 7. c3?! {Too modest. Nothing is contesting the centre, so c4 is free space.} ) ( 7. Ne5?
{The square is not covered yet: ...dxe5 wins a knight for a pawn whichever way White recaptures.}
) 7... Ne4
{The standard Dutch square, and here the pawn on f5 is holding it. That changes what White should do about it. [%weight 6]}
8. h3
{Do not chase it. The knight on e4 is defended by the f5 pawn, so Nc3 only invites ...Nxc3 and a wrecked queenside. Play a move you want anyway and let Black show what the knight is worth. [%csl Rf5,Re4]}
( 8. Nc3?
{The natural challenge, and it is the one move that helps Black: ...Nxc3 leaves White with doubled c-pawns and nothing to show for them.}
) ( 8. Nfd2!?
{The right way to challenge the knight, just a move early. Slipping h3 in first costs nothing and Black cannot improve much in the meantime.}
) ( 8. Ne5?
{Same answer as a move ago: ...dxe5 and White has given a knight for a pawn. The outpost needs a defender first.}
) 8... b6
{Black develops the bishop, which is the piece the Dutch usually struggles with. [%weight 6]}
9. Nfd2
{Now challenge it, and with this knight. Trading on d2 lets White take back with a piece rather than a pawn, so the queenside stays whole. [%cal Gf3d2]}
( 9. Nc3?
{Still the wrong knight. Taking back on c3 with a pawn is exactly what Black wants.} )
9... Nxd2
{Black trades before the knight is pushed back: on e4 it was the best piece, and there was no good way to keep it there. [%weight 7]}
10. Qxd2
{Take with the queen. The knight on b1 stays where it is, the pawns are untouched, and the queen is well placed behind the centre. [%plan Play Nc3 or Nd2 with the other knight, then Rad1 and look for the e4 or d5 break. Black attack needs ...g5, which loosens a king you can already reach down the d-file.] [%line The Dutch, 1...f5]}
( 10. Nxd2!?
{Also fine. The queen recapture keeps the b1 knight free to choose its square later.} ) *


