[Tagline "One setup against almost everything, as White."]
[FormatVersion "1"]
[TagExtensions "vs-c5 vs-qb6 vs-kid vs-gruenfeld vs-bf5 vs-dutch"]
[ChapterId "ch7-tactics-and-plans"]
[ChapterTitle "Typical tactics and plans"]
[DefaultTags "tactics"]

{# Typical tactics and plans

The earlier chapters get you a good position. This one is about what to do with it. Each line below builds a position you already know from another chapter and then asks for one move: the blow that the whole setup was for.

## The e5 outpost

Almost every plan in this repertoire runs through e5. Get a knight there with c3, d4 and the d2 knight behind it, then hold it with f4. When Black trades the knight off, take back with a pawn: the square matters more than the piece, and the pawn that lands on e5 usually attacks something on d6 into the bargain.

## The rook lift

Once the pawn is on f4 the rook on f1 has nowhere to go, so it steps to f3 and swings to h3. It is slow and it is very hard to meet, because Black's pieces are usually on the queenside by then. Bring the cheap pieces first: rook, then queen.

## The h-file

When Black plays ...Nh5 and takes on g3, recapture with the h-pawn. You have swapped a bishop for a knight and been paid an open file at the black king. With that file open, castling queenside is often stronger than castling into it.

## Break timing: c4 and e4

The rule is about Black's d-pawn. If it is on d6, take the centre with c4. If it is on d5, leave the centre closed and play on the kingside instead. Opening the position helps whichever side has the better pieces, and against a fianchetto that is usually not you.

## The Greek gift, Bxh7+

The pattern is worth knowing and worth checking before you play it. Four things have to be true: your bishop bears on h7, a knight can reach g5 straight after, the queen can get to h5, and Black has no knight on f6 to cover h5 and no piece that can drop back to f8 or h7. If any one of those is missing, the sacrifice is just a piece.

The last exercise below is about counting rather than sacrificing, and it is the more useful habit. Bxh7+ turns up there, but it is not a sacrifice at all: the h-file has been open since move six, so the rook behind the bishop covers h7 and the king cannot take. Count the defenders and you find a free pawn. Guess, and you either miss it or throw a bishop away in the next game where the rook is not there.

A true Greek gift, where the piece really is given, is not in the course. None of these lines produced one that the engine agreed with, and a sacrifice nobody has checked is not something to teach.

## Model games

Gata Kamsky is the reference player for this repertoire. His London games show the same handful of ideas over and over: the knight to e5, the slow rook lift, and the willingness to keep the centre closed for twenty moves. Specific games are deliberately not listed here, because the app has no game database to check them against and a citation nobody can verify is worse than none.

## Typical mistakes at this level

Bringing the queen out first and letting it be chased. Sacrificing on h7 without counting the defenders. Opening the centre while your attack is still being built. Playing the fork instead of the threat of the fork, when the threat was doing all the work.}
1. d4 d5 {The most common reply, and where most of these patterns come up. [%weight 8]}
2. Bf4 {The move that defines the opening. [%tags bishop-placement]} 2... e6
{The classical wall goes up: solid, and it shuts in the c8 bishop. [%weight 8]} ( 2... c5
{The immediate strike at d4, the most testing practical try. [%weight 7]} 3. e3
{Hold the centre and finish the setup. [%tags vs-c5]} 3... Nc6
{Development with pressure: the knight leans on d4 at once. [%weight 7]} ( 3... Qb6
{The critical try, hitting b2 where the bishop left it. [%weight 5]} 4. Nc3
{Develop and offer the pawn. [%tags vs-qb6]} 4... Qxb2 {Black takes. [%weight 4]} 5. Nb5
{The knight jumps in, threatening Nc7 forking king and rook. [%tags vs-qb6 tactics]}
5... Na6 {Covering c7, the only tidy way. [%weight 6]} 6. Rb1
{The exercise: bring the last piece in with tempo. The rook attacks the queen on b2 the moment it lands, so Black has to spend another move while White finishes developing. This is what the pawn was sold for: not a forced win, but every move you make comes with a threat and every move Black makes does not. [%tags vs-qb6 tactics] [%plan Meet ...Qxa2 or ...Qb4 by carrying on: Bd3, Nf3, castle, and look for Nc7 or Nd6 once the knight on a6 moves. Play for the initiative and do not count material.] [%line The ...Qb6 grab: trapping her] [%cal Rb1b2]}
( 6. Nc7+?
{It looks like the fork you set up, but the knight on a6 simply takes it. The threat was worth more than the execution.}
) ( 6. Qc1?
{Offering the trade lets Black off the hook and gives back most of the attack. The queen on b2 has no good squares, so keep her there and keep hitting her.}
) ( 6. Bd3?!
{Developing is never bad, and here it costs over a pawn: the rook move develops and threatens at the same time, which is what keeps the queen on b2 in trouble.}
) ) 4. Nf3 {Develop and keep the b1 knight at home for now.} 4... Nf6
{Both sides develop, and Black keeps every setup open. [%weight 7]} 5. c3
{Complete the triangle.} 5... e6
{The solid choice: Black settles into the classical wall. [%weight 7]} 6. Nbd2
{The knight to d2 now that the c-pawn has its square.} 6... Bd6
{The trade offer, exactly as in the classical chapter. [%weight 6]} 7. Bg3
{Keep the bishop. [%tags bishop-placement]} 7... O-O
{Black castles, and the tension on the diagonal stays. [%weight 7]} 8. Bd3
{The bishop takes aim at h7. [%tags bishop-placement]} 8... b6
{The b7 route for the bishop, and queenside play to follow. [%weight 5]} 9. O-O
{King safety first. [%tags king-safety]} 9... Bb7
{The bishop takes the diagonal, and the queenside plan is complete. [%weight 7]} 10. Ne5
{The outpost. [%tags kingside-attack]} 10... Nxe5
{Black trades off the knight rather than living with it. [%weight 6]} 11. dxe5
{The exercise: which pawn takes back? The d-pawn, every time. It rebuilds the pawn on e5 so the square stays White's, and it arrives attacking the bishop on d6, so Black has to move again before doing anything useful. [%tags kingside-attack dark-squares] [%plan After the bishop moves, play f4 to hold e5 and think about Qg4 or Rf3 and Rh3. The pawn on e5 takes f6 and d6 away from Black's pieces, which is worth more than the bishop you did not trade.] [%line The ...c5 lines: the e5 wedge] [%cal Re5d6] [%csl Ge5]}
( 11. Bxe5?
{It trades your best piece and gives the whole advantage back: the pawn recapture is worth about three pawns more, because it keeps the bishop and gains a move.}
) ( 11. f4?
{It hits the knight instead of taking it, and Black answers ...Nxd3, trading the knight for your good bishop. Take the knight first.}
) ) ( 2... Nf6
{The natural developing move, which also brings the ...Nh5 poke within reach. [%weight 8]}
3. e3 {Build the triangle.} 3... e6
{The solid wall, before Black decides whether to poke at the bishop. [%weight 7]} 4. Nf3
{Develop, which also blocks the queen road to h5 and makes ...Nh5 a real move.} 4... Nh5
{The standard poke at the bishop, once Nf3 has covered the queen road to h5. [%weight 5]}
5. Bg3
{Step back and keep the bishop. The knight on f3 is why this works: without it the queen would simply take on h5. [%tags bishop-placement h-file]}
5... Nxg3 {Black takes the bishop, and the h-file opens in White's favour. [%weight 6]}
6. hxg3
{Always the h-pawn. The file opens at Black's king. [%tags h-file kingside-attack]}
6... c5 {Back to the queenside counterplay. [%weight 6]} 7. c3
{Hold the centre before starting anything.} 7... Nc6
{The knight backs the pressure on d4. [%weight 7]} 8. Bd3
{The bishop takes its diagonal. [%tags bishop-placement]} 8... Be7
{The bishop takes the quiet square and Black prepares to castle, into a position where White holds the open h-file. [%weight 6]}
9. Nbd2 {The last minor piece comes out.} 9... O-O
{Black castles into the open file, which is the moment to look for something. [%weight 7]}
10. Bxh7+
{The exercise: count the defenders before you call this a sacrifice. The h-file has been open since move six and the rook on h1 is behind the bishop, so ...Kxh7 is not even a legal move. Black has exactly one reply, and White is a pawn up with the king stripped. [%tags h-file kingside-attack tactics] [%plan After the forced ...Kh8, retreat the bishop to d3 or e4 and bring the knight to e5. You are a pawn up, the black king has no cover, and the h-file is still yours. Do not hunt for a mate: play Ne5, Qg4 or Qf3, and let the extra pawn and the open file do the work.] [%line The h-file: the Greek gift] [%cal Gd3h7,Bh1h7]}
( 10. Qc2?
{The slow build, played without looking at h7. It is a reasonable move in a position that no longer needs one: the pawn was there for the taking.}
) ( 10. Ne5?
{The outpost is right in most positions and it throws this one away: the sacrifice is worth about four pawns and Ne5 keeps half of one. Take on h7 first, and the knight still reaches e5 next move.}
) ( 10. Rxh7?
{The wrong piece. Black takes the rook with the king, because the bishop on d3 is what was covering h7 for the rook, not the other way round.}
) ) 3. e3 {With the bishop already outside, the pawn comes to e3.} 3... Nf6
{Knight out, castling next: the standard developing pattern. [%weight 8]} 4. Nf3
{Develop and take control of e5.} 4... Bd6
{The usual offer to trade off White's good bishop. [%weight 6]} 5. Bg3
{Keep the bishop. [%tags bishop-placement]} 5... O-O
{Black castles and keeps the bishop tension. [%weight 7]} 6. Bd3
{The bishop takes aim at h7. [%tags bishop-placement]} 6... c5
{The standard counter at the base of the triangle. [%weight 6]} 7. c3
{Complete the triangle.} 7... Nc6 {The knight joins the pressure on d4. [%weight 7]}
8. Nbd2 {The queen's knight to d2, keeping c3 for the pawn.} 8... b6
{Black prepares ...Bb7, the standard route out for the light-squared bishop. [%weight 5]}
9. O-O {King safety before the attack. [%tags king-safety]} 9... Bb7
{The bishop reaches the long diagonal, and Black's play stays on the queenside. [%weight 7]}
10. Ne5 {The outpost, with everything behind it. [%tags kingside-attack]} 10... Rc8
{The rook lines up behind the c-pawn, ready for ...cxd4 and the open file. [%weight 5]}
11. f4 {The pawn welds the knight to e5. [%tags kingside-attack]} 11... a6
{Black plays on the queenside, which is where Black's pieces already are. [%weight 4]}
12. Rf3
{The exercise: find the rook lift. With the f-pawn on f4 the rook has nowhere to go along its own file, so it steps up to the third rank and swings across to h3, where it joins the queen against h7. [%tags kingside-attack h-file] [%plan Play Rh3, then bring the queen to h5 or g4. Watch for ...Nxe5: recapture with the f-pawn if the rook has already left f1, and with the d-pawn if you want the e5 square kept.] [%line The classical: the rook lift] [%cal Gf1f3,Rf3h3]}
( 12. Qf3?!
{The queen takes the square the rook wanted. Bring the cheaper piece first: the rook has no other route to the kingside.}
) ( 12. f5?!
{It releases the tension too early. Black answers ...exf5 and the knight on e5 loses the pawn that was holding it.}
) ( 12. Qh5?
{It loses the queen: the knight on f6 plays ...Nxh5 and nothing recaptures. The rook goes to f3 first for exactly this reason, so that the queen arrives with company rather than alone.}
) ( 12. a4!?
{There is nothing wrong with a queenside move, but you have four pieces aimed at the king and Black has none defending it.}
) *
